\begin{document}


\maketitle


\begin{abstract}
  Ensembles of large language models are usually combined by majority voting or a reliability-weighted variant of it, both designed for a single categorical label. Many questions do not have one. Their answers are sets, such as a list of entities or a paragraph containing several separable facts, and an aggregator must decide claim by claim what to keep. We first establish a limit on the standard approach: any aggregation rule that reads only which agents asserted a claim is bounded by a direct estimate of the probability that the claim is correct given that exact asserting subset. That class contains reliability weighting, correlation-aware weighting, and Dawid-Skene annotator models. On QAMPARI the bound is worth $+0.30$ set F1 over plain support counting, with a bootstrap interval of $[-0.34, +0.90]$. We then describe an aggregation template with two task-typed slots: a per-claim confidence signal keyed to the answer format, and a decision rule keyed to the shape of the evaluation metric. Instantiated for list answers, the confidence slot holds the position a claim occupies inside its own asserting agent's output together with how much each silent agent said, and the decision slot holds a per-question keep-count chosen by plug-in expected F1. This reaches $31.70$ F1 against $27.40$ for the best single agent and $29.21$ for multi-agent counting, both significant under a paired bootstrap over questions, and $31.49$ when every training fold excludes the held-out question type. On ASQA the same template loses to reliability weighting at every ensemble size. One statistic computable from cached generations predicts that reversal before any method is built, and a measured absence of the position signal at ASQA response lengths accounts for it.
  % [claim:C001] [evidence:E100]
  % [claim:C002] [evidence:E100,E101]
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{Motivation}

Running several language models on the same question and combining their outputs is now routine. The combination step is usually a vote over whole responses [3] or a learned selection or fusion of them [4]. Both assume the objects being combined are comparable as wholes, so that two responses either agree or do not.

Set-valued answers break that assumption. Asked which manga a given artist drew, a model returns a list. Asked an ambiguous question, it returns a paragraph covering several interpretations. The unit that can be right or wrong is the individual claim inside a response rather than the response itself, and two responses typically overlap in part rather than coinciding. Aggregation must therefore operate at claim level: pool the claims every agent produced, then decide which to keep.

Doing this badly costs more than not aggregating. On our QAMPARI pool, a strict majority vote over pooled claims scores $22.80$ set F1 while the single best agent scores $27.92$, so the ensemble spends eight times the inference budget and loses ground.
% [claim:C003] [evidence:E121]
Response-level selection fails differently. With open-ended answers no two responses coincide, so a global-weight selection rule always returns the highest-weighted agent and reproduces best-single exactly, at $+0.00$ with a zero-width confidence interval.
% [claim:C004] [evidence:E121]

\subsection{Context and the gap}

Three lines of work bear on this problem and none covers it.

\paragraph{Weighted voting over agents.}
Recent work derives provably optimal aggregation weights from higher-order information about agent agreement [2]. The derivation targets binary and categorical predictions. On set-valued answers the published rule is undefined rather than merely weak: the log-odds weight $\log(p / (1-p))$ requires per-agent accuracy above $0.5$, whereas per-claim precision in our pool runs from $5.8\%$ to $28\%$. Every weight is then negative and the optimal threshold switches the filter off.
% [claim:C005] [evidence:E002,E121]

\paragraph{Atomic self-consistency.}
ASC decomposes long-form answers into atomic units, clusters them, and keeps clusters whose member count exceeds a validation-tuned threshold [1]. It handles set-valued answers directly and is the closest published system to ours. Its $m$ samples come from one model, so which agent said a claim does not exist as a variable, and neither does anything about how a particular agent said it.

\paragraph{Claim-level cross-model filtering.}
Systems that check claims from one model against samples from others exist [6], as do multi-agent long-form factuality evaluators [23, 24]. These score or filter claims for factuality assessment. They do not use a claim's placement inside the generating model's own output, treat an agent's silence as graded evidence, or choose how many claims to return.

\paragraph{The gap.}
There is no principled account of how to weight heterogeneous agents when the answers are sets, and no statement of what such weighting can and cannot buy.

\subsection{Insight and research questions}

The entire weighting literature reads one object: the set of agents that asserted a claim. We call it the claim's \emph{support pattern}. Any rule computed from the support pattern alone is a function of that one object. This includes a weighted sum with a threshold, a Naive Bayes model over per-agent true- and false-positive rates, a correlation-aware variant, and a Dawid-Skene annotator model [17]. The best such function is a direct estimate of the probability of correctness conditional on the support pattern, so estimating that quantity and thresholding it bounds the class. Measuring the bound converts an open design space into a single number.

The bound says nothing about a second, disjoint source of information: how each agent presented the claim inside its own output. That is what we exploit.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1.} How much can reliability weighting over agents improve aggregation of set-valued answers, and what bounds it?
\item \textbf{RQ2.} If the bound binds, what information lies outside it, and is that information enough to clear the bound?
\item \textbf{RQ3.} Can we tell in advance, from cached generations and before building anything, which of the two regimes a benchmark is in?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Method summary}

We describe a five-stage aggregation template. Three stages are fixed: pooling claims into candidates, computing the support pattern, and fitting a cross-fitted L2-regularised logistic regression over the assembled features. Two are slots whose contents are determined by a stated property of the task. The \emph{confidence slot} holds whatever per-claim signal the answer format affords. For list answers we fill it with the claim's rank inside each asserting agent's own list, per-agent rank bands, and \emph{omission strength}, which weights each silent agent by the logarithm of how much it said on that question. The \emph{decision slot} holds a rule matched to the metric. For set F1 we use a per-question keep-count given by plug-in expected F1, with the gold-set size supplied by a ridge regression fitted on training questions only.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A ceiling on reliability weighting for set-valued aggregation.} We show by construction that every rule reading only the support pattern is bounded by a direct lookup of the probability of correctness given that pattern, and we measure the bound at $+0.30$ set F1 over plain counting on QAMPARI, with a bootstrap interval of $[-0.34, +0.90]$.
% [claim:C006] [evidence:E100]
\item \textbf{An aggregation template with two task-typed slots}, together with a statement of which property of the task fixes each: the answer format fixes the confidence signal, and the metric fixes the decision rule.
\item \textbf{An instantiation for list answers that clears the ceiling}, using a logistic regression over 92 features and a closed-form keep-count. It reaches $31.70$ F1, which is $+4.30$ over the best single agent and $+2.50$ over counting, both with bootstrap intervals excluding zero.
% [claim:C007] [evidence:E100]
\item \textbf{Two signals for which we found no prior art}: within-output position as a per-claim correctness signal, and graded omission. We report both with their measured limits, including the finding that the position signal is absent on short lists.
\item \textbf{Robustness evidence and a rejected component.} The gain survives holding out an entire question type. Gradient boosting over the same features scores higher in distribution and lower out of it, so we report it as a rejected ablation rather than part of the method.
\item \textbf{A screen that predicts the regime in advance.} The \emph{solo ratio}, defined as the probability of correctness given exactly one asserting agent divided by the probability given at least two, orders our three benchmarks correctly. We show that the two per-ensemble sweeps it predicts are mirror images of each other.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

All notation used later is introduced here.


\paragraph{Task.}
A pool of $n$ agents $M = \{m_1, \dots, m_n\}$ each answers question $q$ once, producing an ordered sequence of claims. Claims are normalised and pooled into a candidate set $C(q)$. A candidate $c$ is \emph{asserted} by agent $m$ if $m$ produced a claim that normalises to $c$. The aggregator outputs a subset of $C(q)$, scored against a gold set $G(q)$.

\paragraph{Support pattern.}
$S(c) \subseteq M$ is the set of agents asserting $c$. Its cardinality $|S(c)|$ is the \emph{support count}. $S(c)$ also determines which agents stayed silent, namely $M \setminus S(c)$.

\paragraph{Within-output position.}
$r_m(c)$ is the 1-indexed position of $c$ in agent $m$'s own output for $q$, defined only for $m \in S(c)$. $L_m(q)$ is the number of claims agent $m$ produced for $q$. Both are properties of the generating agent's output rather than of any list supplied to it.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
On QAMPARI we report set F1 against $G(q)$, macro-averaged over questions. On ASQA we report $\mathrm{DR}^* = \sqrt{\mathrm{ROUGE\text-L} \times \mathrm{STR\text-EM}}$, a proxy for the published DR. The published DR is the geometric mean of ROUGE-L and Disambig-F1, and the ASQA authors state that STR-EM and Disambig-F1 measure the same aspect, which is what licenses the substitution [12]. The proxy avoids standing up a RoBERTa-SQuADv2 reader and is labelled as a proxy throughout.
% [claim:C008] [evidence:E116,E012]
On TriviaQA we report accuracy.

\paragraph{Baselines used as reference points.}
\emph{Best single agent} is the pool member with the highest mean score, selected under the same cross-fitting protocol as every other arm. \emph{Multi-agent count} keeps $c$ if $|S(c)| \geq \theta$, with $\theta$ fitted on training folds; this is our own naive baseline rather than a published method. \emph{Reliability weighting} keeps $c$ if $\sum_{m \in S(c)} w_m \geq \Theta$, with per-agent weights fitted on training folds. The \emph{pattern lookup} keeps $c$ if the training-fold estimate of the probability of correctness given $S(c)$ exceeds a fitted threshold; this is the ceiling of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling}, and in substance a Dawid-Skene annotator model applied to language-model agents [17]. We present it as a baseline drawn from the crowdsourcing literature rather than as our contribution. \emph{Atomic self-consistency} is the published ASC pipeline [1].


\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{The support pattern and its ceiling}
\label{sec:ceiling}

Consider any aggregation rule whose keep or drop decision for candidate $c$ depends on $c$ only through $S(c)$. Weighted voting with per-agent weights is such a rule. So is a Naive Bayes model over per-agent true- and false-positive rates, with or without silence entered as evidence. So is any correlation-aware or diversity-aware weighting, because the pairwise co-assertion structure it reads is itself a function of $S(c)$. So is a Dawid-Skene annotator model [17].

Because the rule sees only $S(c)$, it induces a partition of candidates into at most $2^n$ cells and applies one decision per cell. For any objective monotone in the probability that a kept claim is correct, the best decision within a cell is determined by $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c) = s)$. Estimating that quantity directly on training folds and thresholding it is therefore the best rule in the class, up to estimation error. Support counting is a member of the class, obtained by restricting attention to $|S(c)|$.

Two consequences follow. First, the value of all reliability weighting on a given dataset is a single measurable number: how much the pattern lookup beats plain counting. Second, the argument is a construction rather than an empirical claim, so a negative measurement is informative rather than a failure to find a better weighting scheme.


The construction bounds the \emph{population} optimum of the class. With finite data the lookup must estimate up to $2^n$ cells, so a constrained rule can beat it in sample. We observe this on ASQA (Section~\ref{sec:rq3}) and treat it as a statement about data volume rather than a counterexample to the construction.
% [claim:C009] [evidence:E103]

%% TODO before submission: add one sentence in the body that cites
%% this figure. It is currently placed but never referenced.
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_ceiling.pdf}
  \caption{The support-pattern partition on QAMPARI, which is what the ceiling of this section is made of. Left: each marker is one support pattern $s$, positioned at its support count and its observed correctness rate, with area proportional to the claims it holds; the black line is the rate a rule reading only $|S(c)|$ can see. Any rule in the bounded class chooses one decision per marker, and multi-agent counting chooses one per column, so the vertical spread within a column is exactly the headroom reliability weighting has over counting. Right: where the claims actually are. The takeaway is that the spread is large where the claims are not, reaching 59 points at $|S(c)|=4$ which holds 1,655 claims, against 5.5 points at $|S(c)|=1$ which holds 95,772.}
  \label{fig:ceiling}
\end{figure}


\subsection{A template with two typed slots}

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_template.pdf}
  \caption{The five-stage aggregation template. Stages 1, 2 and 4 are identical across all three benchmarks. Stage 3 holds a per-claim confidence signal fixed by the answer format, and stage 5 holds a decision rule fixed by the shape of the evaluation metric. The takeaway is that one pipeline covers three benchmarks by swapping two slots, rather than three separate methods sharing a family resemblance.}
  \label{fig:template}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:template} shows the pipeline. Stages 1, 2 and 4 are identical across every benchmark we run. Stage 1 varies mechanically with how answers are delimited, using normalised entity strings for list answers, sentence clusters for prose, and distinct terse answers for short-answer QA. Stages 3 and 5 are the two genuine slots, and Table~\ref{tab:slots} states what fixes each.

\begin{table}
  \caption{The two slots and what determines their contents. Stage 3 is fixed by the answer format, because position inside a list exists only when the agent emitted a list. Stage 5 is fixed by the metric, because set F1 admits a keep-count, a recall-only metric does not, and a single-answer metric admits only an argmax. The takeaway is that the task-dependent parts of the method are declared in advance rather than tuned per benchmark.}
  \label{tab:slots}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \toprule
    Stage & QAMPARI & ASQA & TriviaQA \\
    \midrule
    1 pool to units       & entity strings   & sentence clusters  & terse answers      \\
    2 support pattern     & identical        & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{3 confidence signal} & list rank, omission & sentence position & self-consistency \\
    4 cross-fitted logistic & identical      & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{5 decision rule}     & per-question budget & swept threshold & argmax     \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The template carries a precondition, which Section~\ref{sec:screen} makes operational: stage 3 is worth filling only where the ceiling of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} binds.

\subsection{The confidence slot for list answers}
\label{sec:slotA}

For list answers we fill stage 3 with three feature groups. All are computed from the agents' own outputs, and none is a function of $S(c)$ alone. Exact definitions are in Appendix~\ref{app:features}; the feature construction is a single function in the released code.
% [claim:C010] [evidence:E113]

\paragraph{Within-output position.}
For each asserting agent, $\log(1 + r_m(c))$ and the relative position $r_m(c) / L_m(q)$, summarised across asserters by minimum, mean, maximum and standard deviation, plus one per-agent position feature so the model can learn that position means different things for different agents.

\paragraph{Per-agent position bands.}
A one-hot encoding of $r_m(c)$ into seven bands per agent, which lets the scorer express a non-monotone dependence on position without a non-linear model.

\paragraph{Omission strength.}
Every prior rule we are aware of treats silence as one thing. A Naive Bayes model assigns every silent agent the same $\log((1 - \mathrm{TPR}) / (1 - \mathrm{FPR}))$, and a logistic over support indicators assigns it zero. List lengths in this pool span a median of 1 for the smallest agent to a mean of $72.2$ for the largest.
% [claim:C011] [evidence:E107]
An agent that emitted three items and did not mention $c$ has said little about $q$. An agent that emitted fifty and did not mention $c$ has said a great deal about $q$ without saying $c$. We therefore weight each silent agent by $\log L_m(q)$ and enter the sum, the maximum, the count of silent agents with $L_m(q) \geq 20$, and one per-agent term. This is our own construction. We are not aware of prior work that uses it, and we make no claim that prior work supports it.

\paragraph{Stacked pattern probability.}
The training-fold estimate of $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c) = s)$, entered as a single logit-scaled feature, so the scorer receives the ceiling arm's own output as an input rather than competing with it.

\paragraph{Scorer.}
An L2-regularised logistic regression with ridge coefficient $1.0$ over 92 features.
% [claim:C012] [evidence:E113,E114]
Verbosity of the asserting agents is excluded. It adds $+0.01$ over position and is ablated out; the controlled analysis is in Appendix~\ref{app:features}. List length survives only inside omission strength, where a silent agent has no position and length is the only evidence available.

\paragraph{Rejected component.}
Histogram gradient boosting over the same features, with early stopping on a held-out slice of the training fold, produces higher in-distribution scores (Section~\ref{sec:rq2}) and transfers worse (Section~\ref{sec:robust}). It is not part of the method and is reported as the ablation that justifies the choice.

\subsection{The decision slot for set F1}
\label{sec:slotB}

Scoring claims produces an ordering, and something must still decide how far down it to cut. Gold set sizes on QAMPARI range from 5 to 40 with a mean of $10.2$, so a single global threshold is wrong on most questions.
% [claim:C013] [evidence:E112]

For a metric that is an F1 over sets, take the candidates in descending predicted probability $p_1 \geq p_2 \geq \cdots$, substitute $\sum_{i \leq k} p_i$ for the unknown number of correct claims among the top $k$, and substitute $\hat{G}_q$ for the unknown gold size:
\[
  k^*(q) \;=\; \arg\max_k \; \frac{2 \sum_{i \leq k} p_i}{k + \hat{G}_q}.
\]
$\hat{G}_q$ is a ridge regression fitted on training questions only, over question-level quantities available at inference: the number of candidates, the maximum support count on that question, how many candidates have at least two supporters, the sum of predicted probabilities, and the maximum predicted probability.

The estimate need not be accurate. Its mean absolute error is $4.62$ against a gold mean of $10.2$. Substituting the true gold count gains only $+0.55$, and a single constant $k$ fitted across all questions gains nothing over counting, scoring $29.41$ against $29.21$.
% [claim:C014] [evidence:E112,E100]

%% TODO before submission: add one sentence in the body that cites
%% this figure. It is currently placed but never referenced.
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig3_budget.pdf}
  \caption{How the keep-count is chosen, on two held-out questions. The solid curve is the plug-in expected F1 this section maximises, computed from the predicted probabilities and $\hat{G}_q$ alone; the dashed curve is the true F1, which the rule never sees. Left: the argmax lands on the true optimum, and keeping every candidate instead would cost 33 F1. Right: the same rule cuts at 7 against a true optimum of 12 and ends below keeping everything. Note that $\hat{G}_q$ is wrong in both panels, by $+2.4$ and $+1.1$ answers. The takeaway is that the rule needs the curve to have an interior maximum, not the gold-count estimate to be accurate.}
  \label{fig:budget}
\end{figure}

The operative property is that the cut varies per question, not that it is close to correct.


\subsection{Cross-fitting}

Every fitted object is fitted on training folds and applied to held-out questions. This covers the pattern lookup, the logistic coefficients, the gold-count ridge, per-agent weights, and every threshold. We use 5 folds over questions on QAMPARI and 2 on ASQA, where each threshold evaluation costs a ROUGE-L pass. Confidence intervals are paired bootstrap over questions with 10,000 resamples.

This is not a formality on this data. Selecting a keep threshold on the evaluation set rather than on training folds moved one measured gain from $+0.08$ to $-1.25$. % [claim:C015] [evidence:E111]
We therefore treat any in-sample tuned number as uninformative.

\subsection{Screening a dataset before filling the slot}
\label{sec:screen}

The confidence slot is expensive to fill. It requires deciding what signal the format affords, implementing it, and labelling enough claims to fit a scorer. Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} says it is worth filling only where the ceiling binds. The following statistic estimates that from cached generations, with no model calls and no method work:
\[
  \text{solo ratio} \;=\; \frac{P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{exactly 1 agent asserts})}{P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \geq 2 \text{ agents assert})}.
\]
A low ratio means a solo claim is strong evidence of being wrong, so thresholding on support count is already a good filter and weighting has little left to add. That is the regime where the remaining signal must come from elsewhere. A high ratio means counting discards most of the content for little precision gain, so weighting is the only thing that can filter. The share of solo candidates does not carry this information: it is roughly $87\%$ on both QAMPARI and ASQA.
% [claim:C016] [evidence:E118]


\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Benchmarks}

\paragraph{QAMPARI [13].}
777 questions with entity-list answers, scored by set F1. Each list item is natively an atomic fact, so no sentence splitting and no summarisation step is required, and the aggregator's output cannot contain content absent from some agent's response. Grading is programmatic against gold aliases with no judge model. One caveat applies: $97.2\%$ of gold entries ship no alias beyond the answer text, so grading is close to exact string match and correct answers phrased differently score zero. A strict-versus-relaxed sensitivity analysis puts this at roughly $-7$ F1 across every arm, with no conclusion changed, because the penalty is close to uniform.
% [claim:C017] [evidence:E119]
We report it rather than relaxing it silently.

QAMPARI questions come from five structurally different sources (\texttt{wikidata\_simple}, \texttt{wikidata\_intersection}, \texttt{wikidata\_comp}, \texttt{wikitables\_composition}, \texttt{wikitables\_simple}), which we use for the distribution-shift protocol in Section~\ref{sec:robust}.
% [claim:C018] [evidence:E101]

\paragraph{ASQA [12].}
400 ambiguous questions with long-form answers, decomposed into sentence clusters, scored by the $\mathrm{DR}^*$ proxy of Section~\ref{sec:background}.

\paragraph{TriviaQA.}
2,757 short-answer questions, scored by accuracy. Within-output position and the keep-count are both undefined here, because each response is a single answer. TriviaQA enters as a third point for the screen of Section~\ref{sec:screen} and as the benchmark where the confidence slot is filled by self-consistency.

\subsection{Agent pool}

Eight agents on QAMPARI: \texttt{grok-4.3}, \texttt{Kimi-K2.5}, \texttt{MAI-Thinking-1}, \texttt{gpt-5.4}, \texttt{DeepSeek-V4-Flash}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano}, and \texttt{Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026}. Six on ASQA: \texttt{DeepSeek-V4-Flash}, \texttt{MAI-Thinking-1}, \texttt{gpt-5.4}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano}, and \texttt{grok-4.3}. % [claim:C019] [evidence:E115,E104]
Six on TriviaQA. Every agent is sampled once per question at a single decoding setting, and all generations are cached. Every result in this paper is computed from that cache with no further model calls. Table~\ref{tab:lengths} reports the per-agent list-length spread that omission strength depends on.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Per-agent list-length statistics on QAMPARI, which the omission feature relies on. Agents differ by more than an order of magnitude in how much they emit per question. The takeaway is that an agent's silence is gradable rather than binary, which is what makes omission strength a usable signal.}
  \label{tab:lengths}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrrr}
    \toprule
    Agent & Median $L$ & Mean $L$ & Max $L$ \\
    \midrule
    \texttt{Kimi-K2.5}             & 13 & 72.2 & 2431 \\
    \texttt{DeepSeek-V4-Flash}     &  7 & 28.5 & 2398 \\
    \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano}          &  1 & 24.3 & 1067 \\
    \texttt{gpt-5.4}               &  6 & 18.9 &  562 \\
    \texttt{MAI-Thinking-1}        &  8 & 15.3 &  658 \\
    \texttt{grok-4.3}              &  7 & 10.4 &  400 \\
    \texttt{Cohere-command-a-plus} &  3 &  6.7 &  140 \\
    \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini}          &  3 &  4.9 &   77 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C020] [evidence:E107]

\subsection{Baselines}

Beyond the reference points of Section~\ref{sec:background} we run the full reliability-weighting family: marginal per-agent precision, solo precision, Naive Bayes with and without silence as evidence, correlation-aware weighting, and the pattern lookup as the ceiling. We also run the classical rank-fusion rules, namely Reciprocal Rank Fusion [31], Borda, CombSUM and CombMNZ, which a reader familiar with information retrieval will expect to see.

For ASC we run the published list pipeline. We verified against the paper that ASC's list configuration has no LLM composition step, and that its final answer is the list of selected cluster representatives. Our no-composition configuration is therefore the paper's list pipeline rather than a truncation of it.
% [claim:C021] [evidence:E120,E001]
One substitution remains and we state it here. ASC draws $m$ stochastic samples from one model, while our cache holds one response per agent, so agents stand in for samples. That changes the estimand from self-consistency to inter-model agreement. The single-model form is not evaluated here.

\subsection{Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

5-fold cross-fitting over questions on QAMPARI, and 2-fold on ASQA. Paired bootstrap over questions with 10,000 resamples and $95\%$ intervals; $*$ marks an interval excluding zero. The per-ensemble sweeps evaluate all 210 subsets of sizes 3 to 6 on QAMPARI and all 42 subsets of sizes 3 to 6 on ASQA.

\paragraph{One data-hygiene note.}
A subset of generations from one agent contained literal special-token strings that survived into the parsed item lists. Normalisation mapped them to unmatched candidate strings, 1,244 of 110,801 candidates, none of which matched gold. This padded every arm's precision denominator, and more importantly it split candidates that should have merged: a claim emitted by that agent with a leaked prefix and by another agent without one counted as two candidates with support 1 rather than one candidate with support 2. Stripping the tokens leaves 110,136 candidates and changes every arm by at most $0.14$ F1, with no significance verdict altered. The margin of our method over counting grew from $+2.39$ to $+2.50$. All QAMPARI numbers in Section~\ref{sec:results} are post-fix unless explicitly marked otherwise.
% [claim:C022] [evidence:E117,E100]


\section{Results and analysis}
\label{sec:results}

The QAMPARI pool after pooling and normalisation is 110,136 candidate claims across 777 questions, of which $3.57\%$ are correct and 96,548 ($87.6\%$) are asserted by exactly one agent.
% [claim:C023] [evidence:E100]

%% TODO before submission: add one sentence in the body that cites
%% this figure. It is currently placed but never referenced.
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_worked_example.pdf}
  \caption{One held-out question end to end, with every quantity of Section~\ref{sec:background} shown on real data. Rows are the pooled candidate set $C(q)$; a filled cell means that agent asserted the candidate, so the filled cells in a row are the support pattern $S(c)$ and the number inside is the within-output position $r_m(c)$; column headers give each agent's own output length $L_m(q)$; blank cells are the omissions that omission strength grades by $\log L_m(q)$. The takeaway is that support alone would not separate these rows cleanly, since \texttt{barchana} is asserted by four agents and \texttt{jajpur road} by one, yet the resulting ordering keeps all seven gold answers and drops all seven non-answers. This question is favourable; Figure~\ref{fig:budget} shows one where the same rule misses.}
  \label{fig:worked}
\end{figure}


\subsection{RQ1: what reliability weighting is worth, and its bound}
\label{sec:rq1}

\begin{table}
  \caption{QAMPARI main results: 777 questions, 8 agents, 5-fold cross-fitted by question, paired bootstrap over questions with 10,000 resamples. The best rule expressible from the support pattern alone, which upper-bounds every reliability-weighting scheme, beats plain counting by $+0.30$ F1 with an interval of $[-0.34, +0.90]$. The takeaway is that the data place any rule in that class at no more than about $+0.9$ F1 over counting, and that everything above that line comes from information the support pattern does not contain.}
  \label{tab:main}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrll}
    \toprule
    Arm & Set F1 & vs.\ best single & vs.\ count \\
    \midrule
    best single agent (\texttt{Kimi-K2.5})  & 27.40 & (reference)              & $-1.80$ \\
    multi-agent count, $\theta$ cross-fitted & 29.21 & $+1.80\ [+0.76,+2.87]^*$ & (reference) \\
    pattern lookup (ceiling on all weighting) & 29.50 & $+2.10\ [+1.09,+3.09]^*$ & $+0.30\ [-0.34,+0.90]$ \\
    ours: scorer only, global threshold     & 30.68 & $+3.28\ [+2.31,+4.26]^*$ & $+1.47\ [+0.78,+2.14]^*$ \\
    \textbf{ours: scorer + budget}          & \textbf{31.70} & $\mathbf{+4.30\ [+3.37,+5.26]^*}$ & $\mathbf{+2.50\ [+1.72,+3.28]^*}$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C024] [evidence:E100]

Table~\ref{tab:main} answers RQ1, and the answer takes the form of a bound rather than a null: at $95\%$ the data place any rule in the class at no more than about $+0.9$ F1 over plain counting on this benchmark. This is stronger than reporting that the weighting schemes we tried did not work, because the pattern lookup is not one scheme among many. By Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} it bounds the whole class. The bound is on that class's population optimum, and Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} notes that the estimated lookup can be beaten in sample where cells are thin; Section~\ref{sec:rq3} reports the one benchmark where that happens.

The sweep behind the measurement is consistent with it. Correlation and diversity weighting selected $\lambda = 0$ in all five folds, an explicit pairwise co-assertion parameterisation with 28 indicators gained $+0.05$, and question-domain weights gained $+0.00$. % [claim:C025] [evidence:E111]
Those are three different ways of reaching the bound from below.

The support-count profile shows why counting already does most of the available filtering here. $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{exactly } k \text{ agents assert})$ runs $1.2$, $11.1$, $17.4$, $27.2$, $36.5$, $46.3$, $51.9$ and $56.5$ percent for $k = 1 \dots 8$. % [claim:C026] [evidence:E122]
A solo claim is rarely correct, and two agreeing agents raise the odds by roughly an order of magnitude, so a threshold on support count is close to the best this class of information supports.

\subsection{RQ2: what lies outside the bound}
\label{sec:rq2}

The lower two rows of Table~\ref{tab:main} answer RQ2. Adding the confidence slot moves the score from $29.21$ to $30.68$ with a global threshold, and the decision slot adds a further $1.02$ to reach $31.70$. Both increments have bootstrap intervals excluding zero, and the two axes are close to independent. In the ablation sweep the confidence signals alone are worth $+1.44$ over counting, and the budget alone, implemented as \texttt{adapt-count} which uses no agent identity at all, is worth $+1.19$. % [claim:C027] [evidence:E111]
Agent identity is worth roughly $+0.3$ on top of either. The literature this work extends places its weight on agent identity; on this task, the two signals that do not use it are worth roughly five times as much as the one that does.

Within the confidence slot, omission strength is the largest single feature block. Adding it alone is worth $+1.65$ over counting.
% [claim:C028] [evidence:E111]

\paragraph{Ablations, in distribution.}
Gradient boosting over the same features scores $29.95$ alone and $31.73$ with the budget. A logistic and gradient-boosting blend scores $30.80$ and $32.06$. % [claim:C029] [evidence:E100]
The blended arm is the highest in-distribution number we measured, and we do not report it as our result. Section~\ref{sec:robust} says why.

\paragraph{Off-the-shelf fusion.}
Of the classical rank-fusion rules only Reciprocal Rank Fusion beats counting, at $+0.84$. Borda, CombSUM and weighted Borda land between $-0.42$ and $-0.67$, below counting. % [claim:C030] [evidence:E111]
RRF is the strongest parameter-free baseline and we report it as such.

\subsection{Breadth: every ensemble, not one pool}
\label{sec:breadth}

A single 8-agent result invites the objection that the pool was favourable, so we evaluate all 210 subsets of sizes 3 to 6.


