%% this figure. It is currently placed but never referenced.
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig4_mirror.pdf}
  \caption{Gain over multi-agent counting for every agent subset of size 3 to 6, with each point one ensemble and each bar the group median. Counting is the baseline and is drawn at zero. This is Tables~\ref{tab:ens-gain}, \ref{tab:ens-sig} and~\ref{tab:asqa} plotted per ensemble rather than averaged. On QAMPARI the support-pattern ceiling sits above reliability weighting at every size and below the output-structure scorer at every size; reliability weighting is significantly \emph{worse} than counting in 55 of 210 ensembles, a count the tables do not report. On ASQA the ordering reverses at every size. The takeaway is that the two benchmarks give mirror-image verdicts on the same two arms. QAMPARI numbers predate the parsing fix of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}.}
  \label{fig:mirror}
\end{figure}


The size-6 row also settles a question that could be framed as a property of aggregation itself. The point at which weighting stops paying is a property of support-pattern rules specifically. Weighting and the pattern lookup both decay toward zero as the pool grows, and weighting turns negative, while the output-structure arms do not cross over anywhere we can measure.
% [claim:C032] [evidence:E102]

\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robust}

\paragraph{Holding out a whole question type.}
Random folds draw train and test from the same question distribution, so a rule exploiting something peculiar to QAMPARI as a whole would still look fine. We therefore train on four of QAMPARI's five question sources and test on the fifth, five times. Table~\ref{tab:loso} reports the result.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Leave-one-source-out against random 5-fold cross-fitting on QAMPARI. Under a complete change of question type our method loses $0.21$ F1 against a gain of $2.50$, so roughly $92\%$ of the gain survives and it remains significant against counting. The takeaway is that the boosted blend is the best arm under random folds and the worst transfer of any arm, which is why it is not part of the method.}
  \label{tab:loso}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
    \toprule
    Arm & Random 5-fold & LOSO & Shift & LOSO vs.\ count \\
    \midrule
    count                        & 29.21 & 29.21 & $+0.00$ & (reference) \\
    pattern lookup               & 29.50 & 29.44 & $-0.06$ & $+0.24\ [-0.38,+0.80]$ \\
    ours: scorer                 & 30.68 & 30.57 & $-0.11$ & $+1.36\ [+0.66,+2.05]^*$ \\
    \textbf{ours: scorer + budget} & \textbf{31.70} & \textbf{31.49} & $\mathbf{-0.21}$ & $\mathbf{+2.29\ [+1.47,+3.10]^*}$ \\
    logistic/GBM blend + budget  & 32.29 & 31.43 & $-0.86$ & $+2.22\ [+1.40,+3.05]^*$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C033] [evidence:E101]

Two readings matter. First, the extra capacity does not pay out of distribution. The blend gains $+0.36$ over the plain logistic under random folds and loses $0.06$ to it under shift. We take the out-of-distribution ordering as the decision-relevant one, drop boosting from the method, and report the boosted arms as the ablation that justifies the choice.

Second, gains are positive on every held-out source. Per-source gains for the blended arm, with each source held out entirely, run $+1.41$ (\texttt{wikidata\_simple}, $n=327$), $+2.26$ (\texttt{wikidata\_intersection}, $n=164$), $+2.80$ (\texttt{wikidata\_comp}, $n=149$), $+3.31$ (\texttt{wikitables\_composition}, $n=84$) and $+3.73$ (\texttt{wikitables\_simple}, $n=53$). % [claim:C034] [evidence:E101]
The log reports this breakdown for the blended arm rather than for the method as reported, which is a gap we intend to close.

\paragraph{A claim we retract.}
Earlier versions of this work argued that the pattern lookup collapses under this shift, falling from $+0.25$ to $+0.02$, and read that as evidence the protocol detects memorisation while our features survive it. It does not hold. Post-fix the pattern arm moves from $+0.30$ to $+0.24$, a shift of $-0.06$, and under either protocol its interval included zero, so the collapse was movement between two non-significant numbers. The protocol does discriminate, since it separates the boosted arm from the plain one, but it does not indict the pattern lookup and we do not claim that it does. % [claim:C035] [evidence:E101]

\paragraph{How much supervision this needs.}

\begin{table}
  \caption{Gain over counting as a function of training-set size, with a fixed held-out test set and five independent training draws per size, on two different test splits. The gain saturates by roughly 200 questions and the draw-to-draw spread collapses from $\pm 1.64$ to $\pm 0.15$, so past that point it no longer matters which questions were used for fitting. At 25 questions the method loses to counting. The full-data gain here is below the cross-fitted headline of Section~\ref{sec:rq1} and that discrepancy is unresolved.}
  \label{tab:curve}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{rrrr}
    \toprule
    Training questions & Split A & Split B & Spread across draws \\
    \midrule
     25 & $-1.10$ & $+0.18$ & $\pm 1.64$ \\
     50 & $+0.62$ & $-0.20$ & $\pm 0.22$ \\
    100 & $+0.97$ & $+0.54$ & $\pm 0.21$ \\
    200 & $+1.10$ & $+1.00$ & $\pm 0.34$ \\
    350 & $+1.55$ & $+1.11$ & $\pm 0.15$ \\
    527 & $+1.45$ & $+1.28$ & \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C036] [evidence:E108]

The collapsing spread in Table~\ref{tab:curve} answers a specific objection: open-ended questions are all different, so it is unclear how coefficients fitted on one set can transfer to another. The features are normalised by their own question's quantities, so the coefficients describe how these models emit lists rather than the subject matter of any particular question. The measurement is that by $n \approx 200$ the identity of the training questions stops mattering.

\subsection{RQ3: predicting the regime, and the benchmark where we lose}
\label{sec:rq3}

\begin{table}
  \caption{The solo ratio, computed from cached generations alone, against which arm wins on each benchmark. The ratio orders the three benchmarks in the same order as the outcome. Pool heterogeneity does not: the ASQA pool is the most homogeneous of the three, with a coefficient of variation of $0.125$ against QAMPARI's $0.439$, and is where weighting wins by the largest margin. The takeaway is that a one-pass statistic identifies the regime before any method is built.}
  \label{tab:solo}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrl}
    \toprule
    Benchmark & Solo ratio & Which arm wins \\
    \midrule
    QAMPARI  & 0.06 & output-structure signals \\
    TriviaQA & 0.24 & output-structure signals (self-consistency) \\
    ASQA     & 0.47 & reliability weighting \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C037] [evidence:E118]

The strongest form of this evidence is that the two per-ensemble sweeps are mirror images. On QAMPARI our method beats reliability weighting in all 210 ensembles. On ASQA the ordering reverses, as Table~\ref{tab:asqa} shows.

\begin{table}
  \caption{ASQA under the $\mathrm{DR}^*$ proxy, all 42 ensembles of sizes 3 to 6. Our method beats counting everywhere, significantly in 29 of 42 ensembles against weighting's 31 of 42, and loses to reliability weighting at every size with the gap widening as the pool grows. The takeaway is that this is the reverse of Table~\ref{tab:ens-gain} on the same two methods, predicted in advance by Table~\ref{tab:solo}.}
  \label{tab:asqa}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
    \toprule
    Ensemble size & Ensembles & Best single & Count & Weighted & Ours \\
    \midrule
    3 & 20 & 26.58 & 25.88 & \textbf{26.71} & 26.59 \\
    4 & 15 & 26.60 & 25.30 & \textbf{26.74} & 26.51 \\
    5 &  6 & 26.59 & 24.70 & \textbf{26.90} & 26.48 \\
    6 &  1 & 26.53 & 24.27 & \textbf{27.01} & 26.35 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C038] [evidence:E104]

We report this loss as a result rather than a caveat, and we can say why it happens.

\paragraph{The confidence slot is empty on ASQA, and this was predicted before it was measured.}
The position signal on QAMPARI is conditional on list length. Table~\ref{tab:rank} restricts to solo assertions, which removes the support confound, and stratifies by the asserting agent's own list length, which removes the verbosity confound.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Correctness by within-output position on QAMPARI, restricted to solo assertions and stratified by the asserting agent's own list length. On short lists there is no usable position signal, since it is flat or mildly reversed, and the gradient appears only on long lists. The feature pays on QAMPARI because $70\%$ of solo assertions sit in lists of 101 or more items. A control confirms the effect is positional rather than verbosity in disguise: $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{first position})$ by length band is $2.2$, $4.1$, $4.4$ and $4.3$ percent, which is flat. These numbers predate the parsing fix of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}.}
  \label{tab:rank}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrrr}
    \toprule
    Asserter's list length & 1st quintile & Last quintile & Spearman (rank, correct) \\
    \midrule
    1 to 5 items & $2.2\%$ & $4.6\%$ & $+0.084$ \\
    6 to 20      & $5.2\%$ & $2.8\%$ & $-0.048$ \\
    21 to 100    & $5.4\%$ & $1.2\%$ & $-0.102$ \\
    101 or more  & $0.9\%$ & $0.1\%$ & $-0.069$ \\
    pooled solo  &         &         & $-0.121$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C039] [evidence:E105]

ASQA responses average $3.5$ sentences and $79\%$ are 3 or 4 sentences, which is inside the band where QAMPARI shows no signal. We made this prediction before running the measurement and it holds. The Spearman correlation between sentence position and correctness is $-0.029$ in the 3-to-4 sentence band ($n=4{,}064$) and $-0.047$ pooled over solo claims. % [claim:C040] [evidence:E106]
Our method loses on ASQA because the slot it depends on is empty there, not because the template is wrong.

Two further observations sharpen this. First, the decision slot is not the problem: metric-matched budget variants for ASQA score between $24.98$ and $26.70$, all below the swept threshold, so the failure is upstream in stage 3. % [claim:C041] [evidence:E103]
Second, on ASQA reliability weighting scores $27.45$ against the pattern lookup's $27.20$, so the constrained rule beats its own population bound by $+0.25$. With 6,015 clusters and $2^6$ cells the lookup is estimating on thin data, which is what Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} predicts should happen, and it is a second independent reading of the same shortage. % [claim:C042] [evidence:E103]

\paragraph{Three points are an observation, not a law.}
The solo-ratio ordering is consistent across the three benchmarks we have, and it is mechanistically connected to what the counting filter can do. We have not validated it on a benchmark held out from its construction, and we do not claim more than an ordering.

\subsection{Where the method is loose}
\label{sec:calib}

\begin{table}
  \caption{Mean predicted probability against observed correctness, cross-fitted over 110,136 QAMPARI claims. Calibration is close to exact in the bottom bins, which hold $74\%$ of all claims, and overconfident by up to $5.8$ points in the top bin. The takeaway is that the region where calibration is worst is precisely the region the keep-count reads.}
  \label{tab:calib}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
    \toprule
    Predicted $p$ & Claims & Mean predicted & Actual & Gap \\
    \midrule
    0.00 to 0.02 & 81,541 & $0.4\%$  & $0.4\%$  & $0.0$ \\
    0.02 to 0.05 & 12,278 & $3.2\%$  & $4.0\%$  & $+0.8$ \\
    0.05 to 0.10 &  6,925 & $7.1\%$  & $7.5\%$  & $+0.5$ \\
    0.10 to 0.20 &  3,876 & $13.8\%$ & $13.4\%$ & $-0.5$ \\
    0.20 to 0.35 &  2,401 & $26.9\%$ & $26.1\%$ & $-0.9$ \\
    0.35 to 0.50 &  1,602 & $42.1\%$ & $39.5\%$ & $-2.6$ \\
    0.50 to 0.70 &  1,323 & $58.7\%$ & $55.1\%$ & $-3.6$ \\
    0.70 to 1.00 &    190 & $74.8\%$ & $68.9\%$ & $-5.8$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C043] [evidence:E110]

Because the budget substitutes $\sum p_i$ for the unknown number of correct claims, the overconfidence in Table~\ref{tab:calib} makes it overstate expected hits for the kept set and biases $k$ toward keeping too much. This also reframes an earlier finding that isotonic calibration changed nothing: a globally fitted isotonic curve is dominated by the bottom bin, which is already accurate, and barely moves the 3,115 claims above $0.35$ that the decision actually depends on. A recalibration weighted toward the high-$p$ region is untried. It cannot threaten the scorer's own result, because the ordering is calibration-invariant and the $30.68$ row of Table~\ref{tab:main} stands regardless. Only the $+1.02$ the budget adds depends on the sums being right.

\subsection{Negative results}
\label{sec:negative}

We report these because each rules out an approach a reader may otherwise assume was untried. All are cross-fitted against counting on QAMPARI.

\begin{table}
  \caption{Aggregation rules that did not work, with the reading each supports. Several land at or below the ceiling of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling}, which is the behaviour that construction predicts. The takeaway is that the design space around the method has been searched rather than assumed away. These numbers come from the pre-fix sweep and are pending regeneration.}
  \label{tab:negative}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llp{5.4cm}}
    \toprule
    Rule & vs.\ count & Reading \\
    \midrule
    error-correlation weighting   & $\lambda=0$ in 5/5 folds & a function of the support pattern, so Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} caps it \\
    pairwise co-assertion, 28 indicators & $+0.05$ & the same conclusion reached discriminatively \\
    rank agreement across agents  & $+0.07$ & agents agreeing on order adds nothing further \\
    question-domain weights       & $+0.00$ & the five question sources carry no weighting signal \\
    Borda / CombSUM / weighted Borda & $-0.42$ to $-0.67$ & below counting; only RRF survives \\
    within-question $z$-normalisation & $-0.94$ & destroys the cross-question information a global threshold needs \\
    within-question percentile norm.  & $-2.56$ & the same, more severely \\
    learned per-question $k$      & $-3.71$ & $k^*$ is close to unpredictable from question features \\
    single constant $k$           & $+0.20$ & the budget's value is adaptivity, not keeping less \\
    alias merging, six thresholds & $-0.02$ to $-1.30$ & bad merges outnumber good at every threshold \\
    isotonic calibration, global  & $-0.03$ & see Section~\ref{sec:calib} for why this is not the null it appears to be \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C044] [evidence:E111]

The alias-merging row has a clean mechanism. String-similar QAMPARI entities are usually genuinely different entities, such as sequels or people sharing a surname, rather than aliases, and a false merge deletes a correct answer outright because only the cluster representative survives. At a $0.70$ threshold, 14 good merges came with 413 bad ones. % [claim:C045] [evidence:E111]
The same asymmetry explains why replacing edit-distance clustering with an LLM grouping call loses ground: it correctly merges \texttt{Warner Bros} with \texttt{Warner Bros. Pictures} but also merges \texttt{UMass Amherst} with \texttt{University of Nebraska}, at a $7.6\%$ merge rate.
% [claim:C046] [evidence:E123]

One number looks like available headroom and is not. Keeping the best prefix per question, on the ordering the scorer already produces, scores $39.90$. That is the maximum of roughly 143 noisy prefix scores per question and is heavily selection-biased. Every honest attempt to collect it fell far short: a learned $k$ scored $25.64$, a constant $k$ scored $29.41$, and three separate changes to the budget objective each moved the result by at most $0.18$. % [claim:C047] [evidence:E111]
We report it as an artefact of post-hoc selection rather than as a target.


\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Aggregating outputs across multiple models}

The line runs from majority voting over sampled responses [32], through scaling that voting with the number of agents [3], to deriving aggregation weights from higher-order agreement information [2]. A parallel branch selects or fuses whole responses rather than voting, using pairwise ranking with generative fusion [4] or model-based selection of the most consistent response [14]. Theoretical work bounds what any policy whose output is one of the members' answers can achieve [9].

\paragraph{How ours differs.}
This line's estimand is a discrete label or a whole response. Ours is a claim, and our aggregator emits a set assembled from fragments of several responses, so it is not bounded by any single member's answer. We take the weighting question from this line and show that on set-valued answers the entire class it belongs to is bounded, and by how much.

\subsection{Decomposing long-form answers into claims}

Self-consistency over sampled reasoning paths [32] generalises to selecting the most consistent response [14] and then to clustering atomic units within long-form answers and keeping clusters by member count [1]. Atomic decomposition is now general infrastructure for factuality evaluation and calibration [7], including multi-agent evaluators [23, 24, 25] and cross-model consistency checking at claim granularity [6]. A plain union of list answers has been used for biomedical QA [10].

\paragraph{How ours differs.}
All of these draw their samples from one model, or use several models only to check claims, so agent identity does not exist as a variable. We make it available, and one of our findings is that it is worth less than the non-identity signals in the same feature set. We also note that ASC's own paper concedes that model-based decomposition would produce better atomic units than its sentence splitter and was too expensive; our ASQA pipeline shares that limitation.

\subsection{Estimating item-level reliability without labels}

Annotator-reliability models originate in crowdsourcing [17, 18, 19] and have been carried to deep ensembles [20]. A separate strand reads label-free behavioural signals from the model itself, including response length as a hallucination detector [15, 16] and consistency under prompt perturbation as a credibility measure [8, 21, 22]. Position effects in language models are well documented, but on the input side: which of a supplied set of labels a model selects [5], locating items in a given list [26], context position [27], and positional bias when reranking supplied candidates [28, 29]. Adaptive set sizing exists in conformal prediction, targeting coverage rather than F1 [30].

\paragraph{How ours differs, on three axes.}
On granularity, the behavioural-signal work scores whole responses while we score each claim. On position, every result above concerns a sequence given to the model, whereas our signal is the position of an item in the model's own free-form output, which is a different phenomenon; we verified this distinction by reading the nearest work in full rather than inferring it from abstracts [5]. On evidence source, the reliability literature models which agent said what, while we additionally grade silence by how much the silent agent said, which we are not aware of prior work doing.

One directly relevant finding does concern generated position. A study of short-form and long-form factual alignment reports accuracy falling from $51.3\%$ for the first requested fact to $30.1\%$ for the fifth across 16 models [11]. This is in tension with Table~\ref{tab:rank}, which shows no gradient across the first five positions. The likely reconciliation is that their setting requests a fixed number of facts, so later positions are produced under pressure, while our short lists are self-truncated because the model had no more to give. In our long lists, where the model is under enumerative pressure, the gradient reappears. We state this as a hypothesis consistent with both measurements rather than as an established explanation.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Reliability weighting over agents is the standard answer to multi-model aggregation, and on set-valued answers its value is bounded by a quantity that can be measured in one pass. We measured it. On QAMPARI the best rule expressible from the support pattern beats plain counting by $+0.30$ set F1, with an interval that places any rule in the class at no more than about $+0.9$. The information that remains lies in how each agent presented its own answer, specifically where a claim sits in the list that agent produced and how much the agents that omitted it said instead. A scorer over those signals, together with a per-question keep-count matched to the metric, reaches $+4.30$ over the best single agent and $+2.50$ over counting, holds in all 210 agent subsets we tested, and keeps roughly $92\%$ of its gain when the held-out questions are of a type never seen in training.

The template that produces these numbers has two slots rather than one, and each is keyed to a different property of the task. The answer format determines what confidence signal exists, and the metric determines what decision rule is admissible. On ASQA the first slot is empty, we lose to reliability weighting, and both the outcome and the mechanism were predicted in advance, by a ratio computable from cached generations and by a measurement showing the position signal is absent at ASQA response lengths.

When the unit of aggregation stops being the response and becomes the claim, most of the usable signal is not about which model spoke.


\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Scope of the demonstration.}
The confidence slot as instantiated requires list-structured output, and the per-question keep-count requires a metric with a precision term. Neither is defined for single-answer tasks, and on ASQA the confidence slot does not clear the ceiling.

\paragraph{The screen rests on three benchmarks.}
The solo ratio orders them correctly and we give a mechanism, but three points support an observation rather than a law, and none of the three was held out from the screen's construction.

\paragraph{The position signal has a measured domain of validity.}
It is flat or mildly reversed on lists of five items or fewer, and the QAMPARI gain depends on $70\%$ of solo assertions sitting in lists of 101 or more items.

\paragraph{Supervision cost.}
Every arm here is supervised, including all baselines, so the comparison is fair. Our label appetite is larger: 92 coefficients plus a ridge regression, against a single integer for counting. The learning curve places a floor at roughly 50 questions and saturation at roughly 200.

\paragraph{The keep-count is fed by overconfident probabilities} in exactly the region it reads, which biases it toward keeping too much. A high-$p$ recalibration is untried.

\paragraph{The ASC baseline substitutes agents for stochastic samples}, because the cache holds one response per agent. This changes the estimand from self-consistency to inter-model agreement, and the published single-model form is not evaluated.

\paragraph{The ASQA metric is a proxy.}
$\mathrm{DR}^*$ substitutes STR-EM for Disambig-F1. A faithful DR requires a RoBERTa-SQuADv2 reader.

\paragraph{QAMPARI grading is close to exact string match.}
$97.2\%$ of gold entries ship no aliases, costing roughly 7 F1 across every arm approximately uniformly.

\paragraph{All agents are proprietary API models} sampled once at a single decoding setting. We do not measure sensitivity to temperature or to repeated sampling.

\paragraph{Two result tables predate a parsing fix.}
Tables~\ref{tab:ens-gain}, \ref{tab:ens-sig} and \ref{tab:negative} were computed before the normalisation fix described in Section~\ref{sec:protocol} and are pending regeneration. The fix moved every affected QAMPARI arm by at most $0.14$ F1 and altered no significance verdict where it has been rerun, but that is an expectation for these tables rather than a measurement.


